-- A family whose index telescope is DEPENDENT, split at a TIED index — which is
-- what MS9 phase 214 built, and what was refused outright before it.
--
-- `Q`'s second index is typed by its first, so the motive states the second
-- equation only after transporting its side along the first (§3.2 of Pattern
-- Matching Without K). The second equation is therefore stated about the FIRST
-- ONE'S PROOF, and solving the first removes the binder that proof is. The
-- solution transition supplies `refl` for it as it goes, every transport
-- ι-contracts, both equations are discharged, and the arm binds the
-- constructor's arguments alone.
--
-- Compare `dependent-index.thena.script` beside this one: `Below`'s indices are
-- plain variables there, so the dependent one is FRIENDLY and states no
-- equation at all. The difference is the whole of what this file is for.
--
-- And the shape below `tied`, where the first equation is between two
-- CONSTRUCTOR applications so that INJECTIVITY rather than solution fires on
-- it. That one needs no-confusion backwards and its round-trip law, which are
-- generated per datatype since MS9: injectivity replaces the equation's proof
-- with one about the constructor's arguments, the later equation is restated
-- along the proof rebuilt from it, and the law carries the method back.

module TiedIndexChain where

data N : Type₀ where
  z : N
  s : N -> N

data Fin : N -> Type₀ where
  fz : forall (n : N) -> Fin (s n)
  fs : forall (n : N) (i : Fin n) -> Fin (s n)

data Q : forall (n : N) -> Fin n -> Type₀ where
  mk : forall (n : N) (i : Fin n) -> Q n i

-- Both of the split's equations are discharged: the first solves `n`, which
-- supplies `refl` for its own proof, and the second then reads `Eq (Fin (s m)) i
-- (fz m)` with no transport left and solves `i`.
tied : forall (m : N) (x : Q (s m) (fz m)) -> N
tied = \ m x -> case x of { mk n i -> m }

-- INJECTIVITY over the same kind of chain. `isFz`'s target pins the first index
-- to `s n`, so the first equation is `Eq N (s n) (s m)` — two applications of
-- `s`, where `mk`'s above was a variable against a term. Injectivity replaces it
-- with `Eq N n m` and drops its binder, so the second equation, which was stated
-- about that binder, is restated along `noConfusionNInv` of the new proof;
-- `isLeftInvN` is what makes the method's own type and the sub-problem's agree.
-- Both equations are then discharged and the arm binds `n` alone.
data Small : forall (n : N) -> Fin n -> Type₀ where
  isFz : forall (n : N) -> Small (s n) (fz n)

-- The arm answers with the constructor's own `n`, not with the ambient `m`, so
-- reducing it at a known index is what tells the two apart.
tiedTwo : forall (m : N) (x : Small (s m) (fz m)) -> N
tiedTwo = \ m x -> case x of { isFz n -> n }

-- And it reduces through the arm, which is what says the arm was handed the
-- constructor's own `n` rather than the ambient `m`: both are `s (s z)` here,
-- and only reducing tells them apart.
twoFromArm : N
twoFromArm = tiedTwo (s (s z)) (isFz (s (s z)))
